\cf2 Gerry deflected the implied threat by consulting his tablet. As he cradled it in one hand with its bottom edge resting on the table, he tapped and swiped with an air of confidence, a practiced display that he had the measure of any problem even as acids of doubt eroded his insides. \'91Ah!\'92 he bluffed.\
\'91What is it?\'92 Dunhilton relaxed, if only a little.\
\'91This could \'85 yes \'85\'92 Gerry still had nothing.\
\'91Out with it, Gerry.\'92 Dunhilton kept his command as calm as tightening chest would allow.\
Gerry\'92s doubt acids popped a bubble of insight in his racing brain. Or perhaps it was in his equally racing bowels. He clenched as he decided to go with the flow.\
Dunhilton saw a spark of hope. \'91Tax it, Gerry, what\'92ve you got?\'92\
\'91I\'92m looking at Grayling Corporation\'92s Entertainment Lifestyle Vocationally Integrated Servant: User Manual.\'92\
Dunhilton frenetically seized his own tablet to catch up with Gerry\'92s thinking. \'91Yes, yes! What about it?\'92\
\'91I\'92m looking at the Warranty Agreement.\'92\
Dunhilton couldn\'92t admit he wasn\'92t following, but his puzzled look was all Gerry needed.\
\'91Warranty conditions effectively set out performance boundaries of a product,\'92 Gerry explained.\
\'91Well, yes of course,\'92 Dunhilton said, without the lights coming on.\
\'91Proper performance of a product cannot be guaranteed if the conditions are violated. They kinda define the product\'92s weaknesses.\'92\
\'91Weaknesses we can exploit!\'92\
\'91Yes, CEO.\'92\
Dunhilton poked at his tablet like he was gutting a trout. Wanting to get ahead, he ventured, \'91Ah yes, I see. We could fly over the Ghetto and dump bacon and coffee on it. Presumably that\'92ll incapacitate them.\'92\
\'91I like you thinking, your Serene Corporateness, but I\'92m wondering if even the most rancid fat or the hardiest coffee grounds would survive the Layer.\'92\
\'91Right. Keep thinking.\'92\
\'91I never stop, CEO.\'92 Gerry perused and scrolled.\
\'91How could we get them to inhale rhinestones?\'92 Dunhilton wondered, then shook it off and kept reading. \'91How about drugs?\'92\
\'91I rather suspect they are managing that on their own, CEO.\'92\
Dunhilton grunted a reluctant acknowledgment.\
Gerry looked as if he was about to say something, then hesitated a few seconds. \'91I think it comes down to religion, CEO.\'92\
\'91Are you mad? We\'92re in this mess because they\'92re already worshipping Sarah Jacobson as the Holy Momma.\'92\
\'91If there\'92s one thing history has taught us is that the next best thing after having no religion \'85\'92\
\'91Well go on, Gerry.\'92\
\'91Is having at least two religions.\'92\
\'91Are you saying we give them 
\f2\i more
\f1\i0  religion?\'92\
\'91Something they could fight over.\'92\
\'91Well, the Elvi do like a good fight.\'92\
\'91Indeed, CEO.\'92\
\'91And religious wars can go on for centuries.\'92\
\'91Wars can be lucrative.\'92\
\'91Very lucrative,\'92 Dunhilton conceded. \'91But can we contain it?\'92\
\'91Do we need to? We are, after all, above the Layer.\'92\
To Dunhilton that seemed unusually reckless for Gerry. \'91Still, if they fight amongst themselves \'85\'92\
\'91Which we know they have a propensity for.\'92\
\'91Things will go back to normal.\'92\
Gerry scrunched his nose. \'91Tourism won\'92t like it. He wants that Elvi theme park so bad.\'92\
\'91Tax the tourists,\'92 Dunhilton swore, echoing the words of many a political leader down through the ages. \'91But still, it\'92s risky.\'92 Dunhilton looked Gerry straight in the eye as he  reached into his breast pocket and took out his Coins of Allegiance. \'91On the question of whether we use religion against the Elvi: Profits for yes, Losses for no.\'92\
Gerry gave a minimal nod and tightened his lips. Dunhilton tossed the coin high in the air, almost as high as the ceiling. It spun and shimmered as it ascended and fell. It clanked on the table, wobbled and almost rolled off before settling down the farthest end. The two grown men jumped to their feet and scuttled to the end of the table, where the oracle of a tossed coin would decide the fate of the Elvi.\
It came up Profits.\
\'91Religion it is then,\'92 Dunhilton said with relaxed confidence now that matter had been decided. \'91What have you got in mind, Gerry?\'92\
Gerry savoured visions of what he would unleash upon the Elvi and answered, \'91Gospel music.\'92
